.* XLSX00H - help panel group of command XLSX00 (Export to Excel XLSX)
.* Build: CPYFRMSTMF to QTEMP/QPNLSRC(XLSX00H), CRTPNLGRP, CRTCMD HLPPNLGRP
.* (see XLSXDIST.clle).  Lines max 80 columns.  Version 1.4.1 (2026-11-17)
:PNLGRP.
:HELP NAME='XLSX00'.Export to Excel XLSX - Help
:P.The Export to Excel XLSX (XLSX00) command writes the result of an SQL
SELECT statement, or the records of a database file or member, to a
Microsoft Excel workbook (.xlsx, Office Open XML) in a stream file of the
integrated file system.
:P.The workbook is created by an ILE RPG program (XLSX00R) without Java,
PASE or other products. Numbers stay numeric, dates, times and timestamps
become real Excel dates, text is written as Unicode (UTF-8) converted from
the CCSID of each column, and NULL values become empty cells.
:P.A new workbook can be created, a sheet can be added to an existing
workbook, the content of a sheet can be replaced, or rows can be appended
at the bottom of an existing sheet, also in workbooks created by Excel or
other programs.
:P.The written cells can get a workbook name or become an Excel table, and
the sheet can be hidden, so that report sheets, charts and pivot tables of
a template can use the data.
:P.The header row can show the column names, the column headings or the
column text, and the column text can also be added as a second header row
or as Excel notes on the headings.
:P.Version 1.4.1.
:P.:HP2.Restrictions&colon.:EHP2.
:UL.
:LI.
:P.A worksheet holds at most 1,048,576 rows (header included) and 16,384
columns. Rows beyond the limit are not written and the completion message
says that the sheet was truncated. The command handles at most 1000
columns.
:LI.
:P.Text longer than 32,767 bytes is cut. BLOB, ROWID and DATALINK columns
are left empty. CLOB, DBCLOB and XML columns are read up to 32,740 bytes.
:LI.
:P.The workbook must be smaller than 4 GB (no ZIP64). When an existing
workbook is updated, its parts styles.xml, workbook.xml and
[Content_Types].xml must each be smaller than 1 MB.
:LI.
:P.The file is written as a temporary file with the suffix .tmp and renamed
at the end, so an existing workbook is not damaged by a failing run. You
need *RWX authority to the directory of the stream file.
:LI.
:P.The program XLSX00R is called with the library list of the job.
:EUL.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX00/SRC'.SQL or file (SRC) - Help
:XH3.SQL statement or file (SRC)
:P.Specifies what is exported. This is a required parameter.
:PARML.
:PT.:PV.select-statement:EPV.
:PD.An SQL statement that starts with SELECT, WITH or VALUES. System naming
is used: unqualified tables are found through the library list, and both
LIB/TABLE and LIB.TABLE are accepted. Numeric literals use the period as
decimal separator. Column names or AS clauses become the default headings.
:PT.:PV.library-name/file-name:EPV.
:PD.A database file, table or view, as library/file, library.file or
file alone (the file is then found through the library list). A member is
given in parentheses, for example MYLIB/MYFILE(MBR2); the member is read
through the alias QTEMP/XLSX00A.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX00/TOSTMF'.To stream file (TOSTMF) - Help
:XH3.To stream file (TOSTMF)
:P.Specifies the path of the workbook, for example
/home/user/orders.xlsx. This is a required parameter.
:P.With MODE(*REPLACE) an existing file is replaced. With MODE(*ADDSHEET) or
MODE(*APPEND) a file that does not exist is created as a new workbook.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX00/MODE'.Mode (MODE) - Help
:XH3.Mode (MODE)
:P.Specifies how the stream file is written.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*REPLACE:EPK.
:PD.A new workbook with one sheet is created. An existing file is replaced.
:PT.*ADDSHEET
:PD.A new sheet is added at the end of an existing workbook. When the sheet
name is already used, a number is added, for example Data (2).
:PT.*APPEND
:PD.The rows are added after the last row of the sheet named in the SHEET
parameter, or of the last sheet when SHEET(*DFT) is specified. No header
is written unless the sheet is empty; an autofilter of the sheet is
extended to the new rows. When the sheet does not exist, it is added as
with *ADDSHEET.
:PT.*REPLSHEET
:PD.The content of the sheet named in the SHEET parameter is replaced; with
SHEET(*DFT) the file or member name, or Sheet1, is used. The sheet keeps
its name and position; the other sheets, with their formulas, charts and
pivot tables, are kept unchanged and still refer to the sheet by name.
Excel recalculates all formulas when the workbook is opened. Notes,
drawings and tables that were on the replaced sheet itself are dropped.
Pivot tables based on a fixed cell range do not grow with the new rows:
base them on a whole-column range or a dynamic name, and set their option
to refresh data when the file is opened. When the sheet does not exist,
it is added as with *ADDSHEET.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX00/SHEET'.Sheet name (SHEET) - Help
:XH3.Sheet name (SHEET)
:P.Specifies the name of the worksheet (up to 31 characters). The
characters [ ] &colon. * ? / \ are replaced by an underscore.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*DFT:EPK.
:PD.The file or member name is used when a file is exported, otherwise
Sheet1. With MODE(*APPEND) the last sheet of the workbook is used.
With MODE(*REPLSHEET) the sheet with this default name is replaced.
:PT.:PV.sheet-name:EPV.
:PD.Specify the name of the sheet.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX00/HEADER'.Header row (HEADER) - Help
:XH3.Header row (HEADER)
:P.Specifies the content of the header row. The header row is bold on a
blue band; with FILTER(*YES) it has an autofilter and with FREEZE(*YES) it
stays visible when scrolling.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*LABELS:EPK.
:PD.The column headings (LABEL ON COLUMN ... IS, the three lines of 20
characters joined), or the column name when a column has no heading.
:PT.*TEXT
:PD.The column text (LABEL ON COLUMN ... TEXT IS, field text of DDS files),
or the heading, or the name, in this order. The text is taken from the
base column of each result column, also through joins, views and AS
names; expressions use their name.
:PT.*NAMES
:PD.The column names (or AS names) of the result.
:PT.*NONE
:PD.No header row is written. The parameters HDRTEXT, FILTER, FREEZE and
COLDESC are ignored.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX00/HDRTEXT'.Headings (HDRTEXT) - Help
:XH3.Column headings (HDRTEXT)
:P.Specifies your own headings, separated by semicolons, in the order of
the columns. An empty item keeps the heading chosen with the HEADER
parameter. Example&colon. 'Customer;Name;;Balance' changes the headings of
columns 1, 2 and 4.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*NONE:EPK.
:PD.The headings are chosen with the HEADER parameter.
:PT.:PV.heading-list:EPV.
:PD.Specify up to 5000 characters.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX00/FILTER'.Autofilter (FILTER) - Help
:XH3.Autofilter on header row (FILTER)
:P.Specifies whether the header row gets the Excel autofilter buttons.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*YES:EPK.
:PD.An autofilter is set on the header row (on the second header row when
COLDESC(*ROW) or COLDESC(*BOTH) is specified).
:PT.*NO
:PD.No autofilter.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX00/FREEZE'.Freeze header (FREEZE) - Help
:XH3.Freeze header row (FREEZE)
:P.Specifies whether the header rows stay visible when the sheet is
scrolled.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*YES:EPK.
:PD.The header rows are frozen.
:PT.*NO
:PD.The header rows scroll with the data.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX00/LEVEL'.Compression level (LEVEL) - Help
:XH3.Compression level (LEVEL)
:P.Specifies how much effort is spent compressing the workbook. Higher
levels give smaller files and take more time.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.2:EPK.
:PD.Good balance of speed and size, recommended for most exports.
:PT.0
:PD.No compression. Fastest, but the file is 5 to 7 times larger.
:PT.1-9
:PD.Levels 1 to 4 compare each position once (fast). Levels 5 to 9 also
try the next position for a longer match, as zlib does, and give smaller
files. Measured on 200,000 rows by 10 columns (91 MB of sheet data)&colon.
:UL COMPACT.
:LI.
:P.Level 2&colon. 15.1 MB in 6 seconds.
:LI.
:P.Level 4&colon. 12.3 MB in 11 seconds.
:LI.
:P.Level 6&colon. 11.3 MB in 15 seconds.
:LI.
:P.Level 7&colon. 10.8 MB in 19 seconds.
:LI.
:P.Level 9&colon. about as small as level 7, in 33 seconds.
:EUL.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX00/COLDESC'.Column text (COLDESC) - Help
:XH3.Column text&colon. row / notes (COLDESC)
:P.Specifies whether the column text (LABEL ON COLUMN ... TEXT IS, field
text of DDS files) is added to the header. The text is taken from the
base column of each result column. This parameter is ignored with
HEADER(*NONE).
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*NONE:EPK.
:PD.No column text is added.
:PT.*ROW
:PD.A second header row shows the column text, in italics. When the
heading already is the text (HEADER(*TEXT)), the second row shows the
column name.
:PT.*NOTE
:PD.Each heading gets an Excel note, shown when the mouse is over the cell,
with the base column (schema.table.column), the SQL data type, the column
text and the column heading. Notes are added only to sheets created by
the command, not when rows are appended to an existing sheet.
:PT.*BOTH
:PD.Both a second header row and notes.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX00/RANGENAME'.Defined name (RANGENAME) - Help
:XH3.Defined name over the data (RANGENAME)
:P.Specifies a workbook name (Formulas, Name Manager in Excel) for the
cells written by the command&colon. the header row and the data rows, for
example Sales = 'Data'!$A$1&colon.$K$13. Formulas, charts and pivot
tables can use the name instead of a cell range. An existing workbook
name with the same name is replaced; with MODE(*APPEND) only its last row
is moved to the new last row.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*NONE:EPK.
:PD.No name is defined.
:PT.:PV.name:EPV.
:PD.Up to 255 characters&colon. letters A-Z, digits, _ . and \, starting
with a letter, _ or \. A name that looks like a cell reference (for
example AB12 or R1C1) is not allowed. Put the name between apostrophes
to keep lower case letters.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX00/TABLE'.Excel table (TABLE) - Help
:XH3.Excel table (TABLE)
:P.Specifies whether the header row and the data rows of a sheet created
by the command become an Excel table (Insert, Table in Excel). A table has
its own filter buttons and banded rows, and formulas, charts and pivot
tables that refer to the table name follow its size when rows are added.
:P.With MODE(*APPEND), tables of the existing sheet that end at its last
row are extended over the new rows; this parameter is then ignored. With
MODE(*REPLSHEET) the tables of the replaced sheet are removed first, so a
table with the same name can be created again&colon. pivot tables, charts
and formulas of a report template that use the table name keep working
with the new number of rows.
:P.A table needs exactly one header row&colon. it cannot be used with
HEADER(*NONE) or with COLDESC(*ROW) or COLDESC(*BOTH). Duplicate headings
get a number (Name, Name2).
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*NONE:EPK.
:PD.No table; the header row gets a normal autofilter (FILTER parameter).
:PT.*YES
:PD.A table named after the sheet, for example sheet Data gives table Data.
Characters that are not allowed become underscores, and a number is
added when the name is already used.
:PT.:PV.table-name:EPV.
:PD.The name of the table, with the same rules as for RANGENAME. It must
not be used by another table or workbook name.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX00/HIDE'.Hide the sheet (HIDE) - Help
:XH3.Hide the sheet (HIDE)
:P.Specifies whether the sheet written by the command is hidden, for
example a data sheet used by the report sheets of a template. A workbook
must keep at least one visible sheet.
:PARML.
:PT.:PK DEF.*DFT:EPK.
:PD.A new sheet is visible. An existing sheet (MODE(*APPEND) or
MODE(*REPLSHEET)) keeps its state, so a hidden data sheet stays hidden.
:PT.*NO
:PD.The sheet is visible.
:PT.*YES
:PD.The sheet is hidden. It can be shown again with Unhide in Excel.
:PT.*VERY
:PD.The sheet is very hidden&colon. it is not listed by Unhide in Excel and
can only be shown again with VBA or by a program.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX00/COMMAND/EXAMPLES'.Examples - Help
:XH3.Examples for XLSX00
:P.:HP2.Example 1&colon. Export a query:EHP2.
:XMP.
XLSX00 SRC('select * from mylib/orders where year = 2026')
       TOSTMF('/home/user/orders.xlsx')
:EXMP.
:P.Creates orders.xlsx with one sheet, column headings in the header row,
autofilter and frozen header.
:P.:HP2.Example 2&colon. Add a file as a new sheet, with column text:EHP2.
:XMP.
XLSX00 SRC('MYLIB/CUSTOMERS') TOSTMF('/home/user/orders.xlsx')
       MODE(*ADDSHEET) SHEET(Customers) HEADER(*TEXT) COLDESC(*NOTE)
:EXMP.
:P.Adds the sheet Customers to orders.xlsx. The headings are the field
texts; each heading has a note with the field name, type and text.
:P.:HP2.Example 3&colon. Append the rows of a member to a sheet:EHP2.
:XMP.
XLSX00 SRC('MYLIB/DAILY(D20261028)') TOSTMF('/home/user/log.xlsx')
       MODE(*APPEND) SHEET(Daily)
:EXMP.
:P.Adds the records of member D20261028 after the last row of sheet Daily.
:P.:HP2.Example 4&colon. Refresh the data sheet of a report template:EHP2.
:XMP.
XLSX00 SRC('select * from mylib/sales where year = 2026')
       TOSTMF('/reports/sales.xlsx') MODE(*REPLSHEET) SHEET(Data)
:EXMP.
:P.Rewrites sheet Data of sales.xlsx. The summary sheets, charts and pivot
tables that use Data are kept and show the new figures when the workbook
is opened.
:P.:HP2.Example 5&colon. Hidden data table for a report template:EHP2.
:XMP.
XLSX00 SRC('select * from mylib/sales') TOSTMF('/reports/sales.xlsx')
       MODE(*REPLSHEET) SHEET(Data) TABLE(Sales) HIDE(*YES)
:EXMP.
:P.Rewrites the hidden sheet Data as table Sales. Pivot tables and charts
of the template based on Sales show all the new rows.
:P.:HP2.Example 6&colon. Call from RPG:EHP2.
:XMP.
/copy '/home/user/xlsx/XLSX00PR.rpgleinc'
XLSX00R(sql : '/home/user/x.xlsx' : '*REPLACE' : 'Data' : '*TEXT'
        : *omit : '*YES' : '*YES' : 2 : '*NOTE' : rows : msg
        : 'SalesRange' : '*YES' : '*DFT');
:EXMP.
:P.With the optional parameter msg, errors are returned in msg (blank when
the export succeeded) instead of an escape message; rows receives the
number of rows written.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP NAME='XLSX00/ERROR/MESSAGES'.Error messages - Help
:XH3.Error messages for XLSX00
:P.:HP3.*ESCAPE messages:EHP3.
:DL COMPACT.
:DT.CPF9898
:DD.The export failed. The text starts with XLSX00R and tells the
reason, for example an SQL error with its SQLSTATE, a stream file that
cannot be written, or a workbook that cannot be updated.
:EDL.
:P.:HP3.*COMP messages:EHP3.
:DL COMPACT.
:DT.CPF9897
:DD.Number of rows written, sheet, stream file and elapsed seconds.
:EDL.
:EHELP.
:EPNLGRP.
